 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "He was the highest-paid Black entertainer in America. And every night, he had to paint his face black to go on stage.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "America's highest-paid Black star. *Every night, he had to black up.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.3
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "His name was Bert Williams. W. C. Fields called him the funniest man he ever saw, and the saddest man he ever knew.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk4",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "He was born in Nassau, in the Bahamas, in 1874, and grew up in California.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "young1896",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Nassau, Bahamas"
    }
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "walker",
   "text": "In 1893, in a minstrel show in San Francisco, he met George Walker. Together they became the most famous Black performers in the world.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ww",
    "caption": "Bert Williams and George Walker"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1893"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cork",
   "text": "But on stage, they had to wear blackface. Bert was light-skinned. Before every show, he blackened his face with burnt cork.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "cork",
    "focus": [
     0.4,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.45,
     0.5
    ]
   },
   "section": "mask"
  },
  {
   "id": "billing",
   "text": "They even called themselves Two Real Coons, to set themselves apart from the white men in blackface.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "coons",
    "caption": "Williams and Walker poster, 1903"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "dahomey",
   "text": "In 1903, their show In Dahomey became the first all-Black musical at a major Broadway theater. They even played Buckingham Palace.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "dahomey",
    "caption": "In Dahomey, 1903"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1903"
   },
   "section": "fame"
  },
  {
   "id": "seats",
   "text": "Yet inside that theater, Black fans had to sit in a separate section.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar3",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "walkerdies",
   "text": "Then George Walker fell ill. He died in 1911, and Bert was on his own.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "jonah",
    "caption": "Williams and Walker"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ziegfeld",
   "text": "In 1910, Florenz Ziegfeld hired him for the Follies, the biggest show on Broadway. Bert was the only Black star in an all-white cast.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar1",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1910"
   },
   "section": "follies"
  },
  {
   "id": "ultimatum",
   "text": "Cast members demanded that he be fired. Ziegfeld refused. He reportedly told them, I can replace every one of you, except him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "court1",
    "focus": [
     0.25,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.4,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "star",
   "text": "Bert became the star of the show. His sad song Nobody became his signature.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk1",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "paid",
   "text": "He was the highest-paid Black entertainer in America.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "palace",
    "caption": "Palace Theatre poster, about 1918"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "elevator",
   "text": "But when he walked into a hotel, he had to ride the freight elevator.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921b",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.2
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   },
   "section": "insult"
  },
  {
   "id": "applause",
   "text": "He told his friend Eddie Cantor: It wouldn't be so bad, if I didn't still hear the applause ringing in my ears.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "follies1919",
    "caption": "Ziegfeld Follies of 1919, with Eddie Cantor"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bar",
   "text": "Once, a bartender told him a drink would cost fifty dollars. Bert laid a pile of big bills on the bar, and kept ordering.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar2",
    "focus": [
     0.65,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "gambler",
   "text": "In 1916 he filmed his famous poker routine. This is it. One man, no words, a whole card game in his face.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk2",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1916"
   },
   "section": "film"
  },
  {
   "id": "strike",
   "text": "In 1919, Broadway's actors went on strike, and the whole cast walked out. Nobody told Bert. He walked into an empty theater.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "court3",
    "focus": [
     0.25,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.3,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "section": "alone"
  },
  {
   "id": "collapse",
   "text": "In February 1922, sick with pneumonia, he collapsed on stage in Detroit. At first, the audience thought it was part of the act.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "February 1922",
    "sub": "Detroit, Michigan"
   },
   "section": "end"
  },
  {
   "id": "last",
   "text": "Backstage, he reportedly said: That's a nice way to die. They was laughing when I made my last exit.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk6",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "died",
   "text": "On March 4th, 1922, Bert Williams died in New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "uniform",
    "focus": [
     0.45,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.45,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "funeral",
   "text": "Thousands came to see his coffin. He was the first Black man buried with Masonic honors in New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "young1902",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "essay",
   "text": "He once wrote: I have never been able to discover that there was anything disgraceful in being a colored man. But I have often found it inconvenient, in America.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "pk9",
    "focus": [
     0.7,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "close",
   "text": "That was Bert Williams.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "suit1921",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.22
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.36
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Follow The Black History Room for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "section": "cta",
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
